ky to keep if I can't Kong soon.\n\n2. **Play J1**: I have two J1s. This is Red Dragon. Next player discarded J3 earlier, across player discarded J3 and J2 \u2014 dragons are being played cautiously. No one has Peng\u2019d J1 yet. There\u2019s a chance someone is waiting for it. However, since I have two, discarding one reduces risk of giving away a winning tile unless someone has a pair ready. Also, Two Dragon Pungs (6 pts) or Dragon Pung (2 pts) could still apply if I keep it.\n\n3. **Play F1 (East Wind)**: This is the **Prevailing Wind**, so any pung of F1 scores **Prevalent Wind (2 pts)**. Next player has already discarded F1 twice, and across player once \u2014 suggesting they don\u2019t fear it, possibly because they don\u2019t want it. But since it's prevalent wind, it's highly dangerous to discard. Someone might already have a pung concealed. High risk.\n\n4. **Play J3 (White Dragon)**: Only one J3 remains unshown (I hold it). All others are accounted for (3 seen elsewhere). So **no one else can complete a pung/kong of J3**. Therefore, **J3 is completely safe to discard**. Moreover, I only have one, so no value in keeping it.\n\n5. **Play J2 (Green Dragon)**: Next player has Peng\u2019d J2 (so they have 3 exposed), meaning they\u2019ve claimed three J2s. That accounts for all four copies (since there are only four total). Thus, **J2 is completely gone**. I hold one \u2014 but all four are already out: their Peng used three, and I have one. So discarding J2 poses **zero danger** \u2014 it cannot complete any opponent's hand.\n\nWait: Total J2 tiles = 4. Next player has Peng\u2019d J2 \u2192 that\u2019s 3 tiles. I hold 1 \u2192 total 4. So yes, **all J2s are accounted for**. Therefore, **discarding J2 is perfectly safe**.\n\nBetween **J3** and **J2**, both are safe, but **J2 is safer in terms of defensive play** because it\u2019s fully consumed.\n\nBut note: I have **two J1s**. If I discard one J1, I still keep one pair, preserving potential for future pung vi