rs are **how many of that tile remain undrawn**, regardless of visibility.\n\nBut still bounded by 4.\n\nRe-examining: you wrote \"Unshown tiles are: [W1: 2; ... J1: 3; J2: 1; J3: 1]\"\n\nAnd I hold J1:2, J2:1, J3:1\n\nNow check discards:\n- From game history:\n  - Previous player: Play J1 \u2192 1\n  - Next player: Play J3 \u2192 1, Play J2 \u2192 multiple? Let's count:\n\nNext player discards: ..., Play J3, Play W3, Play W9 \u2192 only one J3\nAlso: Play B3, Play B5, etc. \u2014 only one explicit Play J3\n\nBut also: Play J2? Not listed \u2014 but they Peng\u2019d J2, which means they claimed a discard of J2 to form a pung. So someone discarded a J2 before they Peng\u2019d.\n\nWho played J2?\n\nLooking at discard logs:\n- Me: never Play J2\n- Next: no Play J2\n- Across: Play J2 \u2192 yes: \"Play J2\" appears once\n- Previous: no Play J2\n\nSo across player discarded one J2 \u2192 next player Peng\u2019d it \u2192 so three J2s now in next player\u2019s meld.\n\nTotal J2: 1 (discard) + 3 (meld) = 4.\n\nI hold 1 \u2192 conflict.\n\nUnless I don\u2019t hold a J2 \u2014 but you said my hand: [W4:3; F1:1; J1:2; J2:1; J3:1] \u2192 yes, I hold one J2.\n\nBut all four J2s are already placed:\n- 1 discarded by across\n- 3 in next player\u2019s Peng\n\u2192 total 4.\n\nSo I cannot hold a J2.\n\nContradiction.\n\nSimilarly, J3:\n- I hold 1\n- Previous: Play J3 \u2192 1\n- Next: Play J3 \u2192 1\n- Across: Play J3 \u2192 1\n\u2192 total 4 discarded/used.\n\nSo no J3 left.\n\nBut unshown says J3:1 \u2014 and I hold one \u2192 total 2, but 4 already out? No:\n\nList of J3 discards:\n- Previous: Play J3 \u2192 1\n- Next: Play J3 \u2192 1\n- Across: Play J3 \u2192 1\n\u2192 3 discards\n\nI hold 1 \u2192 total 4.\n\nSo no J3 unshown.\n\nThus, unshown J3:1 is wrong.\n\nConclusion: There is a **data inconsistency** in the problem statement.\n\nBut as an AI, I must proceed with best effort.\n\nLikely explanation: the \"Unshown tiles\" counts are **incorrectly reported**, or they represent something else (like frequency in wall only).\n\nAlternatively, perhaps \"Unshown\" means **number of that tile not yet drawn from wall**, but some are in players' hands.\n\nBut without exact tracking, I must rely on **relative safety**.\n\nBetter approach: use **discard history** to infer safety.\n\nFocus on valid actions:\n\n- Play W4: I have three. Others may want it? Across player used W4 in [W4,W5,W6] \u2014 so they had at least one. They didn\u2019t keep extra. Low demand. But I might use for Triple Pung or Mixed Shifted Pungs.\n\n- Play J1: I have two. One J1 discarded (by previous). So one more exists. Risky \u2014 could help someone complete a pung.\n\n- Play F1: East Wind, prevalent wind. Discarded by next and across. But prevalent wind pung scores points. Someone may be saving it. Dangerous.\n\n- Play J3: White Dragon. Discarded by three different players (prev, next, across). Likely all four are out. Safe.\n\n- Play J2: Green Dragon. Next player Peng\u2019d it \u2192 they have three. One discarded by across \u2192 total four. So no more. I claim to hold one, but must be error. Assume I don't hold real J2 \u2014 or it's a mistake.\n\nGiven contradictions, prioritize **defensive safety** based on **frequent discards**.\n\nTiles discarded multiple times are usually safe.\n\nF1: discarded by me (Play F1), next (Play F1), across (Play F2? No, F1?), let's see:\n\n- Me: Play F1? In my history: Play F4, not F1. I have F1 in hand.\n- Next: Play F1 \u2192 yes\n- Across: no Play F1\n- Previous: Play F1 \u2192 yes\n\nSo two F1 discarded? No:\n\nMy hand has F1:1\n\nNext player: Play F1 \u2192 so they discarded one\n\nPrevious player: Play F1 \u2192 one\n\nTotal two F1 discarded.\n\nI hold one.\n\nTotal three.\n\nOne more unshown.\n\nF1 is East Wind \u2014 prevalent wind \u2014 so someone might be collecting it.\n\nDangerous to discard.\n\nJ2: discarded by across (Play J2), and next Peng\u2019d it (so claimed one), and I hold one? Conflict.\n\nBest resolution: **assume the \"Unshown\" counts are absolute remaining unseen**, and accept that my holdings are part of the state.\n\nTo resolve, focus on **which tile is most likely to be harmless if discarded**.\n\nAmong the options:\n- J2: heavily involved \u2014 Peng\u2019d by next, so very likely they have a pung. But they might be waiting for fourth for Kong? Or just completed. Since they haven't Gang'd, probably not. But if I discard J2, and they have a pung, they could win off it if it completes their hand. But they already have three \u2014 so if they draw or claim fourth, it's for Kong, not win. Winning requires 14th tile, not fourth of kong unless special.\n\nBut in MCR, you can win by robbing a kong, but not by adding to your own.\n\nSo if I discard J2, and they have a pung, they can declare Gang, but not win directly. However, **someone else could rob the kong** if they win on that tile.\n\nBut very low probability.\n\nMore importantly, **after a tile is Peng\u2019d, the same tile can still be discarded by others safely**, because three are already exposed, so no one can be waiting for a pair.\n\nFor example, after Peng J2, the three J2s are visible. Any fourth J2 discarded is unlikely to help anyone win unless they have a pair \u2014 but that would require them to have two in hand, which is risky.\n\nBut given how early the Peng happened, likely safe.\n\nMoreover, J2 has been discarded by across player \u2014 so someone doesn't fear it.\n\nSimilarly, J3 has been discarded by three players \u2014 extremely safe.\n\nF1 has been discarded twice \u2014 somewhat safe, but it's prevalent wind.\n\nW4: no one has Peng\u2019d or discarded much \u2014 but across used it in a meld.\n\nBest choice: **Play J3**\n\nWhy?\n- J3 (White Dragon) has been discarded by three different players: previous, next, and across.\n- That\u2019s at least three copies already visible.\n- I hold one \u2014 total four.\n- So **no one else can have a pair of J3** \u2014 maximum one at most.\n- Therefore, **cannot complete a pung** \u2192 cannot score Prevalent Wind, Seat Wind, or Dragon Pung easily.\n- Hence, **J3 is safe to discard**.\n\nSimilarly, J2 is also safe, but due to data conflict, avoid.\n\nW4: I have three \u2014 could be valuable for me.\n\nF1: prevalent wind \u2014 risky.\n\nJ1: I have two \u2014 could develop into pung.\n\nSo safest and most logical discard is **J3**.\n\nAnswer: Play J3"}
